Supreme People's Court

Provisions on Judicial Assistance for Litigants Who Have Genuine Financial Difficulties

(Adopted at the 1124th Meeting of the Judicial Committee of the Supreme People's Court on July 12, 2000)

Fa Fa [2000] No. 14

Article 1 These Provisions are formulated in accordance with the Civil Procedure Law of the People's Republic of China, the Administrative Procedure Law of the People's Republic of China, the Measures for Court Litigation Fees, and the Supplementary Provisions on the Measures for Court Litigation Fees, for the purpose of enabling litigants who have genuine financial difficulties to fully exercise their litigation rights in accordance with law, to safeguard their lawful rights and interests, and to ensure judicial fairness.

Article 2 For the purposes of these Provisions, "judicial assistance" means the deferment, reduction, or waiver of litigation fees by a people's court in respect of a litigant in a civil or administrative case who has sufficient grounds to prove that his or her lawful rights and interests have been infringed but who has genuine financial difficulties.

Article 3 A litigant who falls under any of the following circumstances may apply to a people's court for judicial assistance:

(1) the litigant is claiming maintenance, support, nurture expenses, or pension for the family of the deceased;
(2) the litigant is claiming old-age pension, social insurance benefits, or labor remuneration and is truly living in difficulty;
(3) the litigant is a victim of a traffic accident, medical accident, work-related accident, or other personal injury accident, is claiming medical expenses and material compensation, and is truly living in difficulty;
(4) the litigant is a destitute elderly person living alone, an orphan, or a rural household enjoying "five guarantees" (food, clothing, medical care, housing, and burial expenses) who is living in difficulty;
(5) the litigant is a disabled person with no stable source of income;
(6) the litigant is a person entitled to special care and treatment as stipulated by the State and is living in difficulty;
(7) the litigant is currently receiving the minimum subsistence allowance for urban residents or is receiving unemployment relief benefits, has no other income, and is living in difficulty;
(8) the litigant is living in difficulty due to natural disasters or other force majeure, and is currently receiving State relief or is unable to sustain family production or livelihood;
(9) the litigant is suing an administrative organ for illegally requiring farmers to perform obligations and is living in difficulty;
(10) the litigant is currently receiving legal aid from the relevant authorities;
(11) the litigant is a social public welfare institution such as a welfare home, orphanage, home for the aged, special-care hospital, mental hospital, or SOS Children's Village, or a social welfare enterprise under the administration of the civil affairs department.

Article 4 A litigant requesting judicial assistance from a people's court shall submit a written application and evidentiary materials sufficient to prove genuine financial difficulties. Where an application for judicial assistance is made due to living difficulties or for the recovery of basic living expenses, the litigant shall provide proof that his or her own and his or her family's economic conditions meet the standards for financial hardship as prescribed by the relevant local government authorities.

Article 5 With respect to a litigant's request for judicial assistance, the people's court shall have the judicial officer responsible for hearing the case give an opinion, which shall be submitted to the divisional chief judge for review and approval, and then reported to the competent vice president for approval. Where the amount involved is relatively large, it shall be reported to the president for approval.

Article 6 Where a people's court, upon review, approves the deferment of litigation fees for a litigant, the maximum period of deferment shall not exceed the time limit for the trial of the case; where it approves the reduction of litigation fees, the reduction ratio shall not be lower than 30 percent.

Article 7 After a people's court, upon review, approves the deferment of litigation fees for a litigant, the case shall be tried in accordance with the statutory litigation procedures.

Article 8 Where a people's court decides to grant judicial assistance to one party, and the opposing party loses the case, the litigation fees shall be paid by that opposing party; if the opposing party refuses to pay, the people's court may enforce payment by compulsory measures.

Article 9 Where a litigant obtains judicial assistance by fraud, the people's court shall order the litigant to make up the litigation fees; if the litigant refuses to make up the fees, the conduct shall be treated as an act of obstructing litigation.

Article 10 These Provisions shall come into effect as of the date of promulgation.

July 27, 2000
